WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.485
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.352 --> 00:00:07.810
NICHOLAS CLINTON:
Hello, everybody.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:08.820
My name's Nick Clinton--

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.010
Earth Engine
developer relations.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.110
And we're going to
talk to you today,

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.130
about a project to map
global land cover at higher

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.650
resolution than ever before.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.840
And specifically,
what I mean by that

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.580
is mapping the land cover
in each 30 meter patch

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.520
of the Earth back to 2013.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.590
So this is a very
ambitious project.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:35.340
And this kind of a global land
cover time series has never

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.270
been done before.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.300
So Earth Engine teamed up
with Cloud to make it happen,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.920
and we're going to tell you how.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.900
But first, you
might be wondering--

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.830
what is land cover?

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:51.940
And the analogy that I like
to use is what's on the shelf?

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.350
And so organizations
all over the world--

00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:00.000
governments, NGOs, nonprofits,
researchers, businesses--

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.840
want to understand the literal
landscape in which they're

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.680
doing business.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.690
So here is a more formal
definition of land

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.480
cover-- it's the observed
physical cover of the Earth's

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.480
surface, which describes
the distribution

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.820
of vegetation
types, water bodies,

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:23.910
and human-built environments,
and other land cover types.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.910
That distribution is a map.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:31.320
And so here's a
map of the imagery

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.040
that I showed you a
couple slides ago--

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.600
or rather, the pixels
are turned into a map.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.710
And we'd like to
do this at scale.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:45.330
And do it not only for the
whole surface of the world,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:46.770
but for a time series.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.050
And when you have a time
series of maps like that,

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.320
you can start to
understand processes, like,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.570
the change in crop land,
urbanization, deforestation,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.750
the change in water resources
in your area of interest.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.690
And these are not
just buzz words.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:08.160
Land cover is, in fact,
codified into the UN Sustainable

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.680
Development Goals.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.220
So Google is interested in
helping to achieve these goals.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.030
And one of the key ingredients
to making that happen

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:21.420
is just monitoring
and understanding

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.590
where you are at a given time.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:28.920
And so that's why we
need maps like this.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.890
In order to get a lot
of these at global scale

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:36.975
and a time series of them, we
need to use a lot of imagery.

00:02:36.975 --> 00:02:38.580
And we need to use
a lot of imagery

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.210
to turn all those pixels
into land cover labels--

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.720
like you see in this map.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.610
So fortunately, we have
Earth Engine to help you

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.210
with that kind of thing.

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.350
And what is Earth Engine?

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.870
Earth Engine is
Google's Cloud platform

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.360
for geospatial processing.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.020
And it's got a whole
bunch of good stuff--

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.980
primarily raster data.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.550
So lots of satellite imagery,
but other geospatial data sets,

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:06.790
as well.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:07.900
And here's an example.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.300
This is the same image
that you saw before.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.920
This is Landsat 8, which is the
latest and greatest Landsat.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.820
Landsat 9 is coming.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.660
But we want to turn
all these pixels--

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.860
and we are right up
there, somewhere--

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:28.650
we want to turn all
these pixels into labels.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:35.160
So we have not only images
like that Landsat image,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.410
but we've got lots
of other raster

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.090
and other geospatial
data sets that

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.590
are useful for making
those kind of maps.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:45.870
So we have all of Landsat, all
of Sentinel from the European

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:49.080
Space Agency, lots
of Modus data,

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:54.660
land cover data terrain
data, climate and weather

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.340
data, so on and so forth.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.890
50 plus petabytes of
data in the Earth Engine

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:04.550
catalog and growing daily,
as more scenes come in.

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:09.420
All that data is not useful
without a computation platform.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.010
So the data archive is
co-located with Google scale

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.240
resources to do computation.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.000
And that's what
you need in order

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.149
to tackle big machine
learning problems

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:24.890
like making time series of
those maps all over the world.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:28.070
And to tell you more
about the machine learning

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.500
part of this project is Chris.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:31.820
Take it away, Chris.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:33.800
CHRISTOPHER BROWN:
Thank you, Nick.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.360
Hi, my name is
Chris Brown, and I'm

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.070
an engineer on Earth Engine.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.110
So you're going to hear
a lot about this today.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.340
And I'm not going to
be the first person

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.620
to talk about machine
learning tools at Google.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.440
But we do need to talk about
them here, specifically.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.450
Because to produce
this map, it's

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.225
a more complicated process than
just adding and subtracting

00:04:50.225 --> 00:04:50.930
some pixels.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:53.138
We actually have to have a
little bit of intelligence

00:04:53.138 --> 00:04:53.760
to do this.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.077
So a brief word on some of
the tools we have to build up

00:04:58.077 --> 00:04:58.910
something like this.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:00.702
We could start with
Collab and TensorFlow--

00:05:00.702 --> 00:05:01.830
you all know TensorFlow.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.430
Collab, being an IPython
notebook environment

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.890
that's free, provided
by Google that

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:09.398
has things like TensorFlow
and GPU back ends.

00:05:09.398 --> 00:05:10.940
So we could train
our model this way,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.368
we could build a model this way.

00:05:12.368 --> 00:05:14.660
We can then start to scale
our predictions using things

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.147
like ML Engine, Kubernetes.

00:05:17.147 --> 00:05:19.230
There's an extreme end,
we won't talk about today.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.243
But if you're an app
developer and you

00:05:21.243 --> 00:05:23.660
don't need to create your own
model-- like we did-- you do

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.180
have things like speech
translate and other APIs

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:26.900
built-in.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.317
But what we're
going to talk about

00:05:28.317 --> 00:05:31.280
is how we used things like
TensorFlow, Collab, and then

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.890
AI platform to make this
land cover map happen.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.650
So to build a good
model-- in our case,

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.215
we want to model land cover--

00:05:39.215 --> 00:05:40.340
we need a couple of things.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.200
We need big data.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.780
And Nick was talking
about this 50 petabytes

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.880
and growing of data
in Earth Engine.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:48.150
We need good models--

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.040
this is something that's
brought to bear by TensorFlow.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.020
And then we need a
ton of computation--

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.425
some of which is
on Earth Engine.

00:05:55.425 --> 00:05:57.050
But we can't do it
all in Earth Engine,

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:59.580
and we'll get to
that in a little bit.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.830
So we understand this is a
machine learning problem.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.050
We understand this is
a big data problem.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.280
But we need to talk about why.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:11.937
So if we think for a
second, about land cover--

00:06:11.937 --> 00:06:13.520
we can also get a
little more specific

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.578
and talk about land use.

00:06:15.578 --> 00:06:17.120
So if we think about
land cover being

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.990
what stuff is made of on
the surface of the Earth--

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:24.230
we could use the example of
a parking lot and a road.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.870
They're both made of
asphalt, presumably.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:29.978
And if all you knew was that
this is made of asphalt--

00:06:29.978 --> 00:06:31.520
the way we do it is
with reflectance,

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.100
but bear with me here--
we know material,

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.900
could you tell the difference?

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:36.770
You can't, right?

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.000
It doesn't just matter
what something is made of,

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.960
it's the arrangement of
what it's made of in space.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.140
And so this
transforms our problem

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.610
of identifying land cover from
looking at a single pixel,

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.110
looking at reflectance
saying, oh, I

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:53.505
know what this is to looking at
many, many pixels, and saying,

00:06:53.505 --> 00:06:54.380
we know what this is.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.020
Because we don't just need to
know the reflectances-- a.k.a.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.490
the material at
one point-- we need

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:00.658
to know how that's
arranged in space.

00:07:00.658 --> 00:07:02.450
So this turns this
machine learning problem

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:04.670
into a much heavier problem.

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.635
Our input stopped being
something like red,

00:07:06.635 --> 00:07:08.510
green, blue-- or in our
case, because they're

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:10.520
satellites-- red, green,
blue, ultraviolet, infrared,

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:11.720
and a bunch of other stuff.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.490
To red, green,
blue, ultraviolet,

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:18.590
infrared for 64 by 64 or
greater patches of pixels--

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:21.880
that's a ton of input data.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:25.540
And the question is, do we have
the tools to work with this?

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:26.790
And the answer is we do.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.832
Earth Engine today-- if
you didn't already know--

00:07:28.832 --> 00:07:30.630
does have interoperability
with TensorFlow.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.580
So you can start to
take these images that

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.727
have spatial context
or temporal contexts

00:07:34.727 --> 00:07:36.060
and make predictions about them.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.970
Or run inference
over these images.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:42.070
And this works great, and
well, why don't we stop here?

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:44.370
We're done, that should be it.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:47.077
The problem is you can do
this over a small region.

00:07:47.077 --> 00:07:49.410
You can run TensorFlow on
your machine over these images

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:51.300
and make predictions.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.720
But things get tricky
when you want to scale it.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.000
This is an image where--

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.000
as Nick was describing--

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.362
30 meters per pixel.

00:07:58.362 --> 00:07:59.570
This is of the United States.

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:03.580
And is actually 500 megapixels
or so-- something like that.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.000
And yes, sure, these
aren't just 500 megapixels.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.753
These are 500
megapixels over which,

00:08:08.753 --> 00:08:10.170
a fully convolutional
neural net--

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:11.253
if you know what that is--

00:08:11.253 --> 00:08:12.930
was applied at scale.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.270
And it was done in
about two hours--

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.740
which is amazing.

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:20.830
Because-- bringing this
back to land cover maps--

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:23.070
these are things that
historically take five years

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.320
to build for one year.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.840
And once we talk about
scaling up our ability

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.370
to apply inference--

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:31.470
it's a big data problem.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.000
This isn't just an Earth
Engine large catalog problem,

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.820
this is an Earth Engine
with a big catalog,

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.929
with a big computation problem.

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:39.510
And to actually
scale these types

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.179
of predictions
over the world, we

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:43.659
have to get a
little bit smarter.

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:45.659
And David is going to
tell us how we got smarter

00:08:45.659 --> 00:08:47.810
using Cloud Platform.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:52.240
DAVID CAVAZOS: So now that
we had a trained model,

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.240
we need to do
predictions at scale.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:59.180
So the Earth is big,
it's really big.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:03.970
And we have Landsat 8 satellite
images for the entire world,

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:09.970
for every 16 days since 2013
at that 30 meter resolution--

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:11.540
that's a lot of data.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:13.120
So how do we do
these predictions

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.760
at scale with so much data?

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:19.250
First, we have to subdivide
the world into smaller regions.

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:21.970
Now, the cool thing is that
every individual region

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:23.830
is independent from
each other so they

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:26.260
can be running parallel.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:29.890
So, let's say, we want to
classify this location.

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:32.230
The first step will
be identifying which

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:33.920
region contains this location.

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.370
And then, we can run
the classification only

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:38.000
on that region.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.350
So let's look into more
detail at how we do this.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:45.460
First, we have an App
Engine server into which,

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.720
we submit that location.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:51.250
And then it computes the
bounds of the region that

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:54.100
contains that
location, which is then

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.000
submitted into Earth Engine.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.520
Earth Engine now
extracts this region,

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.860
and it gets stored
in the Cloud Storage.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:04.480
So this is how the Landsat 8
image looks like, or at least,

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:06.250
that's what our eyes can see.

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:08.980
This is the RGB
representation of it.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:12.820
And it's kind of hard
to tell what is what.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:15.130
Everything looks kind
of greenish so we don't

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:18.940
know if it's exactly, like,
grassland, or forest, or even

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:20.740
water.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:24.970
Fortunately, Landsat 8 comes
with very sophisticated sensors

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:29.500
that can see a broader range
of the electromagnetic spectrum

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:32.710
that our eyes cannot see, such
as infrared and even thermal

00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:34.070
information.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:36.910
And all of these
gives a signature

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:41.920
to each of material that
makes it easier for our model

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:46.850
to identify what kind of
material is within each pixel.

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:51.910
So as we can see now in these
other bands of the non-visible

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:52.960
spectrum--

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:56.620
things like water and
concrete stand out a lot more

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:00.120
than in the previous image.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:04.890
So as these Landsat images
are extracted, parts of them

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:06.870
are written into Cloud Storage.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:10.380
As these files are written,
they trigger a Cloud Function

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.200
that does a request to a
Kubernetes cluster running

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:16.540
our TensorFlow model
to do the predictions.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:19.245
And these predictions
are at a file level.

00:11:19.245 --> 00:11:21.780
And once there are
classified, they

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:25.380
are now stored, once
again, into Cloud Storage.

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:28.300
This is what the prediction
for that area looks like.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:30.210
And as we can see,
every single pixel

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:36.370
has a value for one out
of 10 possible classes.

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:39.240
As these land cover files are
written into Cloud Storage,

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:41.460
those trigger another
Cloud Function

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:44.080
that tells our server to
upload that image back

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.170
into Earth Engine.

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:48.270
Where it gets stored
into an image collection,

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:52.440
which contains the
land cover maps

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:57.330
for the entire world for all
the years that we've extracted.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:01.020
Now, these land cover maps are
very useful for decision makers

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:04.170
and other organizations to
make more informed decisions

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.360
about what resources
are in the Earth.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:09.990
And every pixel tells a
story, especially when

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:12.630
we see the change.

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:15.150
Now, one of the very cool
things about Earth Engine

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:18.390
is that once the data
is in Earth Engine,

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:20.910
it's not only visible
within Earth Engine.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:24.960
But it's also accessible to
other external applications,

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:28.830
which made it possible for
us to build a custom web

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:31.260
application using
AngularJS and Maps

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:35.190
API to request both the land
cover images and the landscape

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:35.760
images.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:40.000
And then, render them
into our application.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:43.440
So let's take a look
at some locations.

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:46.490
Here, we are in Cambodia.

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:48.000
What we can see here--

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:51.630
as the dark green--
that's forest.

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:56.720
And all that amber-yellowish
thing-- that's farmland.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:02.340
And if you pay attention to the
central region of that forest--

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:06.690
if we turn on the animation
from 2013 to 2018,

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:09.813
we can see how the
farmland is slowly eating

00:13:09.813 --> 00:13:10.980
through this protected land.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.900
Land cover makes it
easier to quantify.

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:19.050
But we choose the
Landsat images,

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:22.710
then we can clearly see how
this is affecting some very

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:24.660
important habitats in Cambodia.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:31.690
Now we can go into the Amazon.

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:34.180
This is Peru.

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:39.570
And we see this thing that
looks kind of like a river that

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:41.840
is going down.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:45.120
And if we turn the
animation on, then we'll

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.910
see a very interesting
thing-- that this

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:53.840
is growing and flowing, as if
a new river was being created.

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:56.100
Well, this is not
actually a river.

00:13:56.100 --> 00:14:00.000
This is toxic waste and
heavy metal residues

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.510
coming from a mine nearby.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:05.610
Fortunately, some
researchers in Peru

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:10.020
are looking at trees
and plant species that

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:12.450
can grown in these
hazardous environments

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:16.060
to help clean up the area.

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:18.330
And now if we go all
the way in Iran--

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:21.870
this is Lake Urmia, the
largest lake in Iran.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:25.868
And we can see how the
water levels is decreasing.

00:14:25.868 --> 00:14:27.660
And this is due to the
construction of dams

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:30.510
and the effects
of global warming.

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:35.490
In fact, that little green patch
that we see nearby the water--

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:40.130
that used to be an island
surrounded by water.

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:43.910
So as we can see, these land
cover maps tell us a story.

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:48.500
And have a lot of very
insightful information

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:50.630
about the regions
that help us quantify

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:53.120
what's happening in there.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:56.810
But they take a lot of time
and effort to create these.

00:14:56.810 --> 00:15:02.210
In fact, to create a
map of the entire USA,

00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:06.170
it takes at least five years
to create one of these.

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:10.130
So we tried generating the maps
for the entire United States,

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:15.680
and we managed to get the
maps from 2013 to 2018,

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:17.075
in under 24 hours.

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:26.030
So let me show you a
quote from Mahatma Gandhi.

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:28.340
"What we are doing to
the forests in the world

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:30.290
is but a mirror
reflection of what

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:35.060
we are doing to ourselves
and to one another."

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:36.650
But it's not all bad news.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:39.590
There is also, a bunch
of very cool efforts

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:41.090
that are happening
around the world.

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:51.550
Well, something's not working--

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:53.290
there we go.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:57.090
So for example, this is the
Great Green Wall project

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:58.480
that is happening in Africa.

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:01.620
this is in conjunction with a
bunch of different countries

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:03.720
and communities
working together,

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:07.080
to plant a lot of trees
and edible plants.

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:10.980
To create a division between
the now growing Sahara Desert,

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:14.820
and protect the
forests in Africa.

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:18.180
And there are also some
reforestation efforts

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:21.540
in the Amazon, where
people are gathering seeds

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:24.840
from the forests around them
and then mixing them with soil

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:27.420
and planting in affected areas.

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:29.760
Here, we can see
how these plants

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.760
look like after three to
six months of growing.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:34.200
And then these trees--

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:38.790
after five to seven
years of growing.

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:41.070
We all share the same planet.

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:44.070
And every little action-- like,
using less plastic or growing

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:45.030
your own food--

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:47.710
will have an impact.

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:49.350
If you like this
project or would

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:53.220
like to see how it was created,
you can follow this link.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:55.950
And it'll take you
to our GitHub repo.

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:58.740
Or you're can share
this story in Medium.

00:16:58.740 --> 00:16:59.920
Thank you.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:00.420
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

